In the $1^{\text {st }}, 2^{\text {nd }}$ and $3^{\text {rd }}$ transition series, the $3 d, 4 d$ and $5 d$ orbitals are filled, respectively.

Elements in the same vertical column generally have similar electronic configurations. But there are exceptions.

In the first transition series, two elements show unusual electronic configurations:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \operatorname{Cr}(24)=3 d^{5} 4 s^{\prime} \\
& \operatorname{Cu}(29)=3 d^{10} 4 s^{\prime}
\end{aligned}
$$

The second transition series also has exceptions. These are:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \operatorname{Mo}(42)=4 d^{5} 5 s^{1} \\
& \operatorname{Tc}(43)=4 d^{6} 5 s^{1} \\
& \operatorname{Ru}(44)=4 d^{7} 5 s^{1} \\
& \operatorname{Rh}(45)=4 d^{8} 5 s^{1} \\
& \operatorname{Pd}(46)=4 d^{10} 5 s^{0} \\
& \operatorname{Ag}(47)=4 d^{10} 5 s^{1}
\end{aligned}
$$

There are exceptions in the third transition series as well. These are:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{W}(74)=5 d^{4} 6 s^{2} \\
& \mathrm{Pt}(78)=5 d^{9} 6 s^{1} \\
& \mathrm{Au}(79)=5 d^{10} 6 s^{1}
\end{aligned}
$$

Because of these exceptions, the electronic configurations of elements in the same group are often dissimilar.

